        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "refactor(telegram): split bot handler runtime",
        "body": "Closes #106268\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe Telegram bot handler runtime concentrated unrelated authorization, callback, inbound message, migration, reaction, and message-event responsibilities in one 4,200-line module. That made ownership and focused maintenance difficult.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nSplit the runtime into responsibility-owned factory modules while retaining the existing public composer entry point. Registration order, factory scope, behavior, configuration, and APIs remain unchanged.\n\n## User Impact\n\nNo user-visible behavior change. Maintainers get smaller modules with clearer responsibility boundaries.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Blacksmith Testbox through Crabbox: `tbx_01kxdhx6ybfqx079esq1vk72hg`; [Actions run](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/29244467773).\n- `pnpm check:changed -- <eight Telegram runtime files>`: passed formatting, production/test TypeScript, lint, import-cycle, and repository guards.\n- `pnpm test:extension telegram`: 143 files and 2,727 tests passed.\n- Focused baseline and refactor runs: `bot-handlers.runtime.test.ts` plus `bot.test.ts`, 121 tests passed.\n- Fresh autoreview cohorts: no accepted/actionable findings. One unchanged pre-existing replay observation rejected as outside this refactor.\n",
